l Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1045 "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec39ccfa-c0bc-5c06-b1ec-f08770e57898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1061 45414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59e0e71c-41de-59e8-af18-15eaf180ea00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1061 45415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1edc476-8e7a-5e8a-83d5-126f47cd2bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1061 45418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5093fcf-6163-5240-9022-125813bd45bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1061 45419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1821258-7802-571e-a84f-0738986e608c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1061 45420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f254984-e6a9-5246-b99d-34252f2d1d87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1061 45423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c6b333c-1f09-554a-8089-06e97c78f009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1061 45457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "408587e1-74bc-5208-80a7-5977f2bc1961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1064 "The **critical points** of a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ are the points $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist. A critical point such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ is called a **stationary point**.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x,y) = x^2 - 2x + y^2+6y.$ The domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x-2,\n\\qquad\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y+6.\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/9e/9ea3f2d1dd0994be64602aeaffcf5d8424f52daa03844e21e65469f779d9b009.svg)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that ![](images/23/23faa4c01362cad11c2ab6ad19045ca44a09460da1c719c8212480f27f21c5b1.svg) or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist:\n\n- Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2 &=0 \\\\ 2y+6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $x=1$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, $(1,-3)$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\n- $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so there are no more critical points to be found.\n\nTherefore, the only critical point of $f$ is $(1,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1065 7876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "66c2c3d5-6a0a-561b-81c6-ba81419caa61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that, for functions of two variables $f(x,y)$, there are typically three possible types of critical points that can occur:\n\n- Local minimum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ has a minimum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n- Local maximum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = -x^2-y^2$ has a maximum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/008803f43cb2161624f8ef295c70a125b8be64d93184928bd2176a6debaa9f85.png)\n\n- Saddle points. For example, the hyperbolic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2$ has a saddle point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/1214b413073ad87a302fb1297cef47208a922e368d680a9f69ec8fb18d9ca8a0.png)\n\nLocal maximum and local minimum points are analogous to what you would have seen in single-variable calculus. \n\nA saddle point occurs when the slopes are zero in all directions, yet it is not a local maximum or minimum. The hyperbolic paraboloid shown above reaches a local maximum at $(0,0)$ along the $xz$-plane, yet this is a local minimum along the $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1065 7877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe3c5f00-f720-5573-a01f-c37f0a3e447f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Stationary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1064 "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1065 7878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8248e727-7421-5535-ba58-b5ed98f7ac38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1067 "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of a function $f(\\mathbf x).$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that if $H(\\mathbf{a}) = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) \\neq 0$, then we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe second derivatives test follows from these facts. To see why, we first recall Sylvester's criterion:\n\nThe quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 > 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$ and\n\n- negative definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 < 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ are the first and second principle minors of $A,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice the following:\n\n- The first principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta_1 = (\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}))_{11} = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}.\n$$\n\n- The second principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is the Hessian determinant\n\n$$\n\\Delta_2 = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) = H(\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can go through each case:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 < 0.$ Hence, by Sylvester's criterion, the Hessian matrix $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive definite nor negative definite, and we conclude that $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 > 0.$ Then:\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} < 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} > 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1065 7886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0bcd9133-9025-5637-8956-90a3b84934a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1067 "Relating the Second Partial Derivatives Test to the Taylor Polynomial Classification" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238338 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local maximum at $(3,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238338 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238338 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2083fb67-822f-5c61-a364-c7c9d194031b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238456 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238456 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238456 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d567df9-25a8-5d23-8121-d47039a7f3a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238920 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6248101c-5576-5662-a6e6-9e82ed11cdba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239356 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(5,-4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9657dc67-31a7-5307-b0a3-970644a18023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239522 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local minimum at $(3,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a885e205-57e3-5ddd-bba6-1c3c5468b96a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186238456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2d816319-57a5-5bfb-bdbc-1674489ced59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9136d3e6-f2a0-5b9c-a6a2-efe0a42ad6ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241041 #edb/ref 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x &=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452755 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452756 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452757 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(1,1)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5521ee3b-fffd-5558-98b1-b81f3a08c9ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351244 #edb/ref 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399468 #edb/ref 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y &=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399469 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399470 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399470 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399470 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399471 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399471 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399471 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" #edb/